Description: Do not dump core in timeout when it passes on the signal which killed the command.
 When the command it runs is killed by a signal, timeout kills itself with the same signal, so that its own exit
 status shows the signal. For a signal like SIGSEGV or SIGABRT, timeout then dumped core too, and apport reported a
 crash of timeout besides the crash of the command. timeout now first turns off core dumps for itself, with
 prctl(PR_SET_DUMPABLE, 0), or where that fails with a core size limit of 0, and kills itself only when that succeeded,
 as GNU timeout does. The new test test_child_core_dump_not_repeated runs timeout with an unlimited core size and checks
 that it is killed by SIGSEGV without dumping core.
 .
 Both commits of the pull request, combined. The test is placed before test_preserve_status_even_when_send_signal, as
 0.10.0 lacks the test which precedes it upstream.
